## Analysis

**Interpreting the hypothesis:** The condition states that for all $\gamma > 0$, there exists $m_\gamma$ such that for all $n > m_\gamma$:
$$\frac{1}{x_n^\gamma} < n \implies x_n^\gamma > \frac{1}{n} \implies x_n > n^{-1/\gamma}.$$

(The expression $1/x_n^\gamma$ being well-defined for all real $\gamma > 0$ implies $x_n > 0$.)

**Key observation:** The hypothesis holds for *all* $\gamma > 0$, so in particular it holds for $\gamma = 1$. This alone gives us: there exists $m_1 \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for all $n > m_1$:
$$x_n > \frac{1}{n}.$$

## Proof

The statement is **TRUE**.

Since the hypothesis holds for every $\gamma > 0$, take $\gamma = 1$. There exists $m_1 \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for all $n > m_1$:
$$\frac{1}{x_n} < n, \quad \text{i.e.,} \quad x_n > \frac{1}{n}.$$

Now fix any $c > 1$ (for concreteness, $c = 2$). For all $n > m_1$:
$$c^n x_n > \frac{c^n}{n}.$$

Since $c > 1$, we compute:
$$\log\!\left(\frac{c^n}{n}\right) = n \log c - \log n \xrightarrow{n \to \infty} +\infty,$$
because $n \log c$ grows linearly while $\log n$ grows only logarithmically. Therefore:
$$\frac{c^n}{n} \to +\infty \quad \text{as } n \to \infty.$$

Consequently, $c^n x_n \to +\infty \neq 0$. The general term of the series $\sum_{n \geq 1} c^n x_n$ does not tend to zero, so by the **divergence test** the series diverges (indeed, diverges to $+\infty$).

Thus there exists $c > 1$ such that $\sum_{n \geq 1} c^n x_n = \infty$ — in fact, **every** $c > 1$ works. $\blacksquare$

